"Buy Canadian update: strategic procurement threshold lowered to $5 million | CanadaBuys (https://canadabuys.canada.ca/en/news-and-events/buy-canadian-update-strategic-procurement-threshold-lowered)\nciteturn37view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn32view1\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 65\nL0: Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website.\nL1: \nL2:   * cite0†Skip to main content L3:   * cite1†Skip to \"About Canada.ca\" L4: \nL5: ## Language selection\nL6: \nL7:   * cite2†fr (Français) L8: \nL9: Search CanadaBuys [Input: Search CanadaBuys]\nL10: \nL11: Search\nL12: \nL13: ## Canada.ca Menu\nL14: \nL15: Main Menu\nL16: \nL17: ## Buy Canadian Policy\nL18: \nL19: The Government of Canada has introduced new policies to protect, build, and transform Canadian industries. Check out cite3†Buy Canadian Policy for more information on what this could mean for you.\nL20: # Buy Canadian update: strategic procurement threshold lowered to $5 million\nL21: \nL22: [Button: Print]\nL23: \nL24: * * *\nL25: \nL26: June 16, 2026\nL27: As of June 15, 2026, the threshold of application under the cite4†Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Suppliers and Canadian Content in Strategic Federal Procurements has been lowered from $25 million or more to $5 million or more. This change expands the policy’s application to more federal procurements, supporting broader opportunities for Canadian suppliers and encouraging greater Canadian content.\nL28: The threshold under the cite5†Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Materials in Federal Procurements remains unchanged at $25 million or more.\nL29: ## Get assistance\nL30: \nL31: For more information about the Buy Canadian Procurement Policy Framework, please visit cite3†Buy Canadian Policy and consult the cite6†Questions and answers for suppliers .\nL32: \nL33: If you need help understanding the federal procurement process, please visit cite7†Procurement Assistance Canada†www.canada.ca .\nL34: \nL35: cite8†Image L36: \nL37: cite9† Report a problem on this page L38: \nL39: Date modified:\nL40:     2026-06-17\nL41: ## CanadaBuys Links\nL42: \nL43:   * cite10†News and events L44:   * cite11†Buyandsell†buyandsell.gc.ca L45:   * cite12†System alerts L46:   * cite13†Procurement and contracting data L47:   * cite14†Sitemap L48: ## Canada.ca Links\nL49: \nL50:   * cite15†Contact Support†www.canada.ca L51:   * cite16†News†www.canada.ca L52:   * cite17†Prime Minister†pm.gc.ca L53:   * cite18†Departments and agencies†www.canada.ca L54:   * cite19†Treaties, laws and regulations†www.canada.ca L55:   * cite20†How government works†www.canada.ca L56:   * cite21†Public service and military†www.canada.ca L57:   * cite22†Government-wide reporting†www.canada.ca L58:   * cite23†Open government†ouvert.canada.ca L59: \nL60: ## About Canada.ca\nL61: \nL62: cite0†Top of Page L63: \nL64: cite24†Image: Symbol of the Government of Canada --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAccess to Information Act (https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/A-1/section-86.html?wbdisable=true)\nciteturn37view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn32view2\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 162\nL0:   * cite0†Skip to main content L1:   * cite1†Skip to \"About government\" L2:   * cite2†Switch to basic HTML version L3: \nL4: ## Language selection\nL5: \nL6:   * cite3†Français L7: \nL8: ## Search\nL9: \nL10: Search Canada.ca [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input: Search Canada.ca] [Input]\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: \nL14: ## Menu\nL15: \nL16: Main Menu\nL17: \nL18: ## You are here:\nL19: # Access to Information Act (R.S.C. (Revised Statutes of Canada), 1985, c. A-1)\nL20: \nL21: Full Document:\nL22: \nL23:   * cite4†HTMLFull Document: Access to Information Act (Accessibility Buttons available) |\nL24:   * cite5†XMLFull Document: Access to Information Act [477 KB] |\nL25:   * cite6†PDFFull Document: Access to Information Act [933 KB]\nL26: \nL27: Act current to 2026-09-21 and cite7†last amended on 2026-06-14. cite8†Previous Versions L28: \nL29: cite9†Go to full page cite10†Table of Contents L30: \nL31: Marginal note:Contracts over $10,000\nL32:   * 86 (1) Within 30 days after the end of each of the first three quarters and within 60 days after the end of each fourth quarter, the head of a government entity shall cause to be published in electronic form the following information with respect to any contract that is entered into during the quarter in relation to the activities of the government entity if the value of the contract is more than $10,000:\nL33: \nL34:     * (a) the subject matter of the contract;\nL35: \nL36:     * (b) the names of the parties;\nL37:     * (c) the contract period;\nL38: \nL39:     * (d) the value of the contract;\nL40: \nL41:     * (e) the reference number assigned to the contract, if any; and\nL42: \nL43:     * (f) any other information that, in accordance with Treasury Board policies, must be published.\nL44: \nL45:   * Marginal note:Contracts of $10,000 or less\nL46: (2) Within 30 days after the end of the quarter in which a contract that has been entered into in relation to the activities of a government entity and that has a value of $10,000 or less is amended so that its value exceeds $10,000, or within 60 days after the end of that quarter if that quarter is the fourth quarter, the head of the government entity shall cause to be published in electronic form the information referred to in paragraphs (1)(a) to (f) with respect to the contract as amended.\nL47:   * Marginal note:Increase or decrease in value of contract\nL48: \nL49: (3) Within 30 days after the end of the quarter in which a contract referred to in subsection (1) or (2) is amended so that its value is increased or decreased by more than $10,000, or within 60 days after the end of that quarter if that quarter is the fourth quarter, the head of the government entity shall cause to be published in electronic form the value of the contract as amended.\nL50: \nL51:   * cite11†2019, c. 18, s. 37 L52: ## Table of Contents\nL53:   * cite12†Access to Information Act L54:     * 1 - cite13†Short Title L55:     * 2 - cite14†Purpose of Act L56:     * 3 - cite15†Interpretation L57:     * 3.2 - cite16†Designation L58:     * 4 - cite17†PART 1 - Access to Government Records L59:       * 4 - cite18†Access L60:         * 4 - cite19†Right of Access L61:         * 5 - cite20†Information about Government Institutions L62:         * 6 - cite21†Requests for Access L63:         * 12 - cite22†Access Given L64:       * 13 - cite23†Exemptions L65:         * 13 - cite24†Responsibilities of Government L66:         * 19 - cite25†Personal Information L67:         * 20 - cite26†Third Party Information L68:         * 21 - cite27†Operations of Government L69:         * 24 - cite28†Statutory Prohibitions L70:         * 26 - cite29†Refusal of Access L71:       * 27 - cite30†Third Party Intervention L72:       * 30 - cite31†Complaints L73:       * 32 - cite32†Investigations L74:       * 36.1 - cite33†Orders and Reports L75:       * 38 - cite34†Reports to Parliament L76:       * 41 - cite35†Review by the Federal Court L77:       * 54 - cite36†Office of the Information Commissioner L78:         * 54 - cite37†Information Commissioner L79:         * 56 - cite38†Assistant Information Commissioner L80:         * 58 - cite39†Staff L81:         * 59 - cite40†Delegation L82:         * 60 - cite41†General L83:       * 67 - cite42†Offences L84:       * 68 - cite43†Exclusions L85:       * 70 - cite44†Designated Minister’s Duties and Functions L86:       * 71 - cite45†Regulations L87:     * 71.01 - cite46†PART 2 - Proactive Publication of Information L88:       * 71.01 - cite47†Senate, House of Commons and Parliamentary Entities L89:         * 71.01 - cite48†Interpretation L90:         * 71.02 - cite49†Senators L91:         * 71.05 - cite50†Members of the House of Commons L92:         * 71.08 - cite51†Parliamentary Entities L93:         * 71.12 - cite52†Parliamentary Privilege and Security of Persons, Infrastructure and Goods L94:       * 72 - cite53†Ministers L95:       * 81 - cite54†Government Institutions L96:       * 90.01 - cite55†Office of the Registrar of the Supreme Court of Canada, Courts Administration Service and Office of the Commissioner for Federal Judicial Affairs L97:         * 90.01 - cite56†Interpretation L98:         * 90.02 - cite57†Office of the Registrar of the Supreme Court of Canada L99:         * 90.1 - cite58†Courts Administration Service L100:         * 90.14 - cite59†Office of the Commissioner for Federal Judicial Affairs L101:         * 90.22 - cite60†Judicial Independence, Protected Information, Security of Persons, Infrastructure and Goods and Canadian Judicial Council L102:       * 91 - cite61†General L103:     * 92 - cite62†PART 3 - General L104:     * cite63†SCHEDULE I - Government Institutions L105:     * cite64†SCHEDULE II L106: Select page[Select][Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL107: ## Page Details\nL108: \nL109: Date modified:\nL110:     ﻿2026-09-28\nL111: \nL112: ### Justice Laws Website\nL113: \nL114:   * cite65†Constitutional Documents L115:   * cite66†Related Resources L116:   * cite67†Consolidated Acts and Regulations L117:   * cite68†Search L118:   * cite69†Help L119:   * cite70†Contact Us†www.justice.gc.ca L120: \nL121: ### Department of Justice Canada\nL122: \nL123:   * cite71†Family Law†www.justice.gc.ca L124:   * cite72†Criminal Justice†www.justice.gc.ca L125:   * cite73†Funding†www.justice.gc.ca L126:   * cite74†Canada's System of Justice†www.justice.gc.ca L127:   * cite75†Laws L128: ### Government of Canada\nL129: \nL130:   * cite76†All contacts†www.canada.ca L131:   * cite77†Departments and agencies†www.canada.ca L132:   * cite78†About government†www.canada.ca L133: #### Themes and topics\nL134:   * cite79†Jobs†www.canada.ca L135:   * cite80†Immigration and citizenship†www.canada.ca L136:   * cite81†Travel and tourism†travel.gc.ca L137:   * cite82†Business†www.canada.ca L138:   * cite83†Benefits†www.canada.ca L139:   * cite84†Health†www.canada.ca L140:   * cite85†Taxes†www.canada.ca L141:   * cite86†Environment and natural resources†www.canada.ca L142:   * cite87†National security and defence†www.canada.ca L143:   * cite88†Culture, history and sport†www.canada.ca L144:   * cite89†Policing, justice and emergencies†www.canada.ca L145:   * cite90†Transport and infrastructure†www.canada.ca L146:   * cite91†Canada and the world†www.international.gc.ca L147:   * cite92†Money and finances†www.canada.ca L148:   * cite93†Science and innovation†www.canada.ca L149:   * cite94†Indigenous Peoples†www.canada.ca L150:   * cite95†Veterans and military†www.canada.ca L151:   * cite96†Youth†www.canada.ca L152:   * cite97†Manage life events†www.canada.ca L153:   * cite98†Social media†www.canada.ca L154:   * cite99†Mobile applications†www.canada.ca L155:   * cite100†About Canada.ca†www.canada.ca L156:   * cite101†Terms and conditions†www.canada.ca L157:   * cite102†Privacy†www.canada.ca L158: \nL159: cite0†Top of Page L160: \nL161: cite103†Image: Symbol of the Government of Canada†wet-boew.github.io --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContracting Policy Notice 2025-1: Amendments to the Government Contracts Regulations - Canada.ca (https://www.canada.ca/en/treasury-board-secretariat/services/policy-notice/2025-1.html)\nciteturn37view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn32view3\",\"lineno\":38}); Total lines: 103\nL0:   * cite0†Skip to main content L1:   * cite1†Skip to \"About government\" L2: \nL3: ## Language selection\nL4: \nL5:   * cite2†fr (Français) L6: \nL7: cite3†/ Gouvernement du Canada L8: \nL9: ## Search\nL10: \nL11: Search Canada.ca [Input: Search Canada.ca]\nL12: \nL13: Search\nL14: \nL15: * * *\nL16: \nL17: ## Menu\nL18: \nL19: Main Menu\nL20: \nL21: ## You are here:\nL22: # Contracting Policy Notice 2025-1: Amendments to the Government Contracts Regulations\nL23: \nL24: Date: January 2, 2025 (Updated January 9, 2026)\nL25: \nL26: To: Senior Designated Officials for the Management of Procurement and Functional Heads of Procurement, Administration and Finance units at all departments\nL27: \nL28: Subject: Amendments to the cite4†Government Contracts Regulations†laws-lois.justice.gc.ca L29: ## Overview\nL30: \nL31: The Governor in Council has approved changes to the cite4†Government Contracts Regulations†laws-lois.justice.gc.ca (the regulations), which came into effect on December 16, 2024. The amendments and their explanations are detailed in the cite5†Canada Gazette†canadagazette.gc.ca . These amendments clarify longstanding procurement policy and practice.\nL32: \nL33: The following types of contracts are now explicitly excluded from the requirements of the regulations:\nL34:   1. A contract between the Government of Canada and a government entity (meaning a government of a province, a foreign state, a political subdivision of a foreign state, or a municipality; a Crown corporation or a corporation or agency wholly owned by one of these governments; or an intergovernmental organization established by two or more states, or an agency of such an organization).\nL35:   2. A contract where a national security exception (NSE) is properly invoked in accordance with section 10(3) of the cite6†Canadian International Trade Tribunal Procurement Inquiry Regulations†laws-lois.justice.gc.ca , or where the procedure set out in subsection 10(3) has been followed for those procurements indispensable to national security that are not covered by a trade agreement.\nL36: ## Updates new\nL37: \nL38: The cite7†Directive on the Management of Procurement†www.tbs-sct.canada.ca was amended to reinforce obligations for ensuring that accurate and comprehensive procurement records applicable to the procurement file are created and maintained, including when invoking an NSE.\nL39: Contracting authorities are responsible for including the approval letter signed by the appropriate assistant deputy minister, the rationale for invoking an NSE, and the justification for the selected procurement strategy in the procurement file (section  4.10.1.11).\nL40: When the information security categorization of the NSE documentation exceeds that of the procurement file, officers should include in the procurement file a reference note that confirms that the NSE was invoked. The note should cite the approving authority and the date of approval and should indicate that full supporting documentation is retained separately. The full NSE documentation should be stored in an approved secure repository in accordance with departmental security protocols.\nL41: ## Application\nL42: \nL43: As with all exempted contracts in section 3 of the regulations, the deemed terms (section 18 of the regulations) continue to apply.\nL44: Section 10(3) of the cite6†Canadian International Trade Tribunal Procurement Inquiry Regulations†laws-lois.justice.gc.ca sets out that a NSE is properly invoked when it has been approved in writing by an assistant deputy minister, or a person of equivalent rank, who is responsible for awarding the designated contract prior to the day on which the contract is awarded.\nL45: Departments continue to be required to maintain the government’s commitment to fairness, openness and transparency in the bidding process for contracts. Where a NSE is properly invoked, departments are expected to continue to prioritize consideration of competitive procurement strategies, to the maximum extent possible, while protecting the national security interests of Canada.\nL46: Departments are reminded of their obligations to ensure that decisions related to procurement, including decisions to use a non-competitive process, are fully justified and documented. Accurate and complete procurement documentation must be created and maintained in the contract file.\nL47: As with any procurement, departments are required to ensure that proper controls, oversight and processes are in place for contracting. Departments are advised to update their departmental procurement management framework to reflect the amendments.\nL48: ## Enquiries\nL49: \nL50: Questions about this policy notice or the regulations should be directed to TBS Public Enquiries (questions@tbs-sct.gc.ca).\nL51: \nL52: Samantha Tattersall\nL53: Assistant Comptroller General, Investment Management Sector\nL54: Treasury Board of Canada Secretariat\nL55: \nL56: © His Majesty the King in Right of Canada, as represented by the President of the Treasury Board, 2025\nL57: ISSN: 1491-591X\nL58: \nL59: ## Page details\nL60: \nL61: 2026-01-09\nL62: \nL63: ## About this site\nL64: \nL65: ### Treasury Board of Canada Secretariat (TBS)\nL66: \nL67:   * cite8†Contact us L68:   * cite9†Forms L69: ### Government of Canada\nL70: \nL71:   * cite10†All contacts L72:   * cite11†Departments and agencies L73:   * cite12†About government L74: #### Themes and topics\nL75:   * cite13†Jobs L76:   * cite14†Immigration and citizenship L77:   * cite15†Travel and tourism†travel.gc.ca L78:   * cite16†Business L79:   * cite17†Benefits L80:   * cite18†Health L81:   * cite19†Taxes L82:   * cite20†Environment and natural resources L83:   * cite21†National security and defence L84:   * cite22†Culture, history and sport L85:   * cite23†Policing, justice and emergencies L86:   * cite24†Transport and infrastructure L87:   * cite25†Canada and the world†www.international.gc.ca L88:   * cite26†Money and finances L89:   * cite27†Science and innovation L90:   * cite28†Indigenous Peoples L91:   * cite29†Veterans and military L92:   * cite30†Youth L93:   * cite31†Manage life events L94: ### Government of Canada Corporate\nL95: \nL96:   * cite32†Social media L97:   * cite33†Mobile applications L98:   * cite34†About Canada.ca L99:   * cite35†Terms and conditions L100:   * cite36†Privacy L101: \nL102: cite37†Image: Symbol of the Government of Canada "
